  "markdown_report": "The best initial direction is a **paired temporal-sensitivity audit of the supplied GRU4Rec**, establishing whether recent interactions influence target ranking before changing training. Research progress is 0%, and no measured diagnostic results were supplied.\n\n**Starting insights.** First, existing margins measure training-pair separation. Their dependence on score scale prevents interpreting larger margins as improved HR@10. Second, the feedback distinguishes recent coherent interests from broad historical relevance. This motivates temporal perturbations, but does not establish users’ current intent. Third, available histories support controlled sequence comparisons. Unavailable ownership, mood, ratings and compatibility facts must remain unknown.\n\n**Related-work insights.** First, [Does It Look Sequential?](https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.12008) reports dataset-dependent effects of shuffling. Accuracy and recommendation stability therefore warrant separate measurement. Second, [Hidasi and Czapp](https://hidasi.eu/assets/pdf/eval_flaws_recsys23.pdf) document disagreement between sampled and full-catalog evaluation. Diagnostic rankings cannot replace the pinned selection objective. Third, [FActScore](https://aclanthology.org/2023.emnlp-main.741/) supports decomposing claims and checking evidence. Here, this motivates reporting observable sensitivity separately from speculative rejection explanations.\n\nThese insights define three directions: temporal dependence, ranking reliability, and evidence-grounded interpretation. The framework below separates observable measurements from their interpretive limits.\n\n| Direction | Measurement | Remaining gap |\n|---|---|---|\n| Temporal dependence | Paired target-rank changes | Sensitivity does not establish useful intent |\n| Ranking reliability | Fixed candidates; frequency strata | Sampled ranks do not establish catalog performance |\n| Grounded interpretation | Metric definitions; explicit unknowns | Implicit histories cannot confirm rejection reasons |\n\nThree atomic ideas follow. Scores are originality/potential/code difficulty, each out of ten; higher difficulty means harder implementation.\n\n1. **Matched recent-versus-old deletion audit — 4/7/3.** Originality: a useful controlled adaptation, although perturbations are established. Potential: directly tests the stated temporal hypothesis, although effects may reflect distribution shift. Difficulty: requires only inference, although batching needs care. Related work: Does It Look Sequential?\n2. **Frequency-stratified ranking audit — 3/6/2.** Originality: exposes hidden aggregate behavior, although stratification is standard. Potential: identifies sampling-sensitive failures, although it does not correct them. Difficulty: simple training-frequency counts, although small strata yield uncertainty. Related work: Hidasi and Czapp.\n3. **Evidence-tagged diagnostic interpretation — 4/6/3.** Originality: connects grounding to recommendation diagnosis, although the grounding principle is established. Potential: reduces unsupported conclusions, although it cannot recover missing preferences. Difficulty: straightforward schema changes, although semantic verification remains challenging. Related work: FActScore.\n\nSelect idea 1 for feasibility. For training-history prefix targets, compare original histories with equal-sized recent and oldest deletions using identical candidates. Report paired rank changes, diagnostic HR@10, counts and user-bootstrap intervals. Preserve left padding, final-nonpadding readout, all four category routes and existing adapter hooks. Keep the trained checkpoints fixed. Select subsequent training changes exclusively by pinned macro validation HR@10; no improvement is claimed here.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Add a paired temporal-sensitivity probe to the corrected GRU4Rec diagnostic adapter, comparing the ranking impact of deleting recent versus oldest interactions.",
    "motivation": "The qualitative feedback suggests that broad historical relevance may displace recent coherent interests, while existing embedding and margin probes cannot measure temporal sensitivity. This audit tests an observable implication without assuming latent intent or changing the model family.",
    "implementation_notes": "Retain DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). Preserve existing metrics and ModelView interfaces. Construct diagnostic examples solely from user_train histories: use each eligible user's final training interaction as the diagnostic target and preceding interactions as context; this is an in-sample sensitivity audit, not an unseen validation estimate. Require at least four context items. After applying the baseline context-length cap, set k=max(1,floor(context_length/4)); remove either the k most recent or k oldest context events. Left-pad every variant to the same length. Read features at the greatest index whose input ID is nonzero, never count-1. Use predict_score for all candidate scoring, without assuming dot-product output heads. Draw 99 distinct real-item negatives excluding the user's complete training history, using a local seed-42 generator; skip and count users lacking enough eligible negatives. Reuse candidates across variants. Aggregate per user before category summaries and bootstrap paired users separately within each category for an equal-weight macro diagnostic difference. Report missing measurements as null with counts and reasons. Explicitly label results as conditional on trained checkpoints and sampled candidates. Preserve and restore model training mode; do not modify weights or global random state. The interpretation prompt must include measured metrics and definitions and prohibit inferring ownership, mood, compatibility or confirmed rejection reasons. This diagnostic-only change leaves checkpoint and embedded predictor architecture unchanged: each category continues to load exactly one independently trained GRU4Rec checkpoint.",
    "pseudocode": "class DiagnosisProbe:\n    def run_full_diagnosis(self, train_loader):\n        original_mode = self.view.model.training\n        self.view.model.eval()\n        try:\n            with torch.no_grad():\n                metrics, definitions = existing_family_aware_probes(train_loader)\n                records = []\n                rng = local_generator(seed=42)\n                for user, history in deterministic_training_history_subset():\n                    context, target = history[:-1], history[-1]\n                    context = context[-baseline_max_length:]\n                    if len(context) < 4:\n                        record_skip('short_context'); continue\n                    negatives = sample_unique_unseen_training_items(user, 99, rng)\n                    if negatives is None:\n                        record_skip('insufficient_negatives'); continue\n                    candidates = [target] + negatives\n                    k = max(1, len(context) // 4)\n                    variants = [context, context[:-k], context[k:]]\n                    ranks = []\n                    for events in variants:\n                        sequence = left_pad(events, baseline_max_length)\n                        features = self.view.log2feats(sequence)\n                        last = greatest_nonzero_input_index(sequence)\n                        logits = self.view.predict_score(features[:, last], candidates)\n                        ranks.append(target_rank_with_fixed_tie_rule(logits))\n                    records.append(per_user_paired_rank_and_hit_changes(ranks))\n                metrics.update(summarize_with_paired_user_bootstrap(records, seed=42))\n                definitions.update(temporal_probe_definitions_and_limitations())\n                return {'metrics': metrics, 'metric_definitions': definitions}\n        finally:\n            self.view.model.train(original_mode)\n\ndef diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis):\n    return evidence_grounded_prompt(raw_diagnosis['metrics'], raw_diagnosis['metric_definitions'])",
    "originality": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "Equal-sized temporal deletions provide a focused adaptation of sequence perturbation analysis to the supplied failure hypothesis.",
      "negative": "Sequence ablations and paired ranking comparisons are established methods."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 7,
      "positive": "The audit can guide later GRU4Rec recency-training experiments while exposing category-specific behavior.",
      "negative": "Sensitivity alone cannot demonstrate better recommendations or identify causal user preferences."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 3,
      "positive": "Uses existing histories, inference hooks and checkpoints without retraining.",
      "negative": "Candidate exclusion, padding, score shapes and user-level uncertainty require careful implementation."
    }
  },
